 "title": "Change of Subject of a Formula",
 "seo_title": "Change of Subject – JSS 3 Mathematics Worksheets with Answers (NERDC)",
 "short_title": "Change of Subject",
 "domain": "Algebraic Processes",
 "standard_code": "JSS 3: Algebraic Processes",
 "standard_url": "https://nerdc.gov.ng/",
 "standard_text": "Substitute values into formulae; make a given letter the subject of a formula. (summary of the NERDC JSS 3 Mathematics curriculum)",
 "meta_desc": "Free JSS 3 maths worksheets on formulae: substituting values and changing the subject of a formula, including formulas with squares and fractions. 100 questions with answers.",
 "key_big": "Undo the operations in reverse order",
 "key_sub": "Do the same to both sides.",
 "body_html": "<h2>How to think about it</h2><ul><li>The subject is the letter on its own on one side: in v = u + at, v is the subject.</li><li>To change the subject, undo the operations on the new subject, doing the same to both sides.</li><li>Example: v = u + at → v − u = at → t = (v − u) ÷ a.</li></ul><h2>Examples</h2><div class='sn-ex'><span class='sn-ex-t'>Example</span><p class='sn-ex-q'>Make r the subject of A = πr²</p><p class='sn-ex-s'>r² = A ÷ π</p><p class='sn-ex-a'>Answer: r = √(A ÷ π)</p></div><h2>Common mistakes</h2><div class='sn-tw'><table class='sn-table'><thead><tr><th>Mistake</th><th>What to do instead</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Undoing in the wrong order</td><td>Undo + and − first.</td></tr><tr><td>Changing only one side</td><td>Keep the balance.</td></tr><tr><td>Forgetting brackets</td><td>t = (v − u) ÷ a, not v − u ÷ a.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>",
 "faq": [
  {
   "q": "What is a formula?",
   "a": "A rule that connects quantities, such as A = lb."
  },
  {
   "q": "Why change the subject?",
   "a": "To work out a different quantity."
  },
  {
   "q": "What is the inverse of squaring?",
   "a": "Square root."
  }
 ],
